  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "A lead lost in the sixth. That's how fragile the margin is right now.",
    "what_this_means": "Shea Langeliers made it matter in the seventh. A single that flipped a 3\u20132 deficit into a go-ahead moment. The lineup was there when it mattered. Ten hits across the game, eight strikeouts, one run scored on runners in scoring position. That conversion rate is the story. They had the game through the middle innings. St. Louis came back in the sixth. Then the Athletics answered. But Jack Perkins couldn't hold it. Two runs in the ninth ended it. A one-run loss at home is not a disaster. A one-run loss after you had it is exactly the kind of game that stacks into a streak. Inconsistency doesn't feel different when you're living it. It feels like bad luck. It's not. They were winning. They stopped. Three lead changes in one game means the margin is whoever finishes sharper. Today that was St. Louis.",
    "what_to_watch": "SF brings Tyler Mahle to the mound tonight. A 5.18 ERA over the season. And Aaron Civale takes the ball for Oakland, coming off four straight starts where his ERA sits at 1.71. That matchup tilts toward advantage when the starter performs.",
